<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Assembly (Hungary)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Országgyűlés</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Assembly is the single-chamber parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, meeting in the Parliament building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>. Its history reaches back to the medieval diet, and since the free elections of 1990 it has elected the prime minister and the president. The 2011 Fundamental Law, adopted by a two-thirds majority, reduced its size to 199 members.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-hungary.jpg" alt="National Assembly (Hungary)" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Imperator125 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hungarian_parliament_2026_-_2030.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, called the Országgyűlés, was a legislative institution of the medieval kingdom of Hungary from the 1290s, and continued in its successor states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> kingdom of Hungary. Its name in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> was Parlamentum, and the word Diet came into use mostly in the early modern period. It met at regular intervals with interruptions between 1527 and 1918, and again until 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1608 a two-part legislature was enacted as the Royal Hungarian Diet. The upper board held high nobles and high priests, the archbishops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. The lower board, or board of envoys, held representatives of the common nobility, the clergy and the free royal cities. About 10 per cent of the voting-age population could vote for its delegates, 5 per cent of them county nobility and 5 per cent residents of free royal cities. The whole body numbered about 500 people in the 17th and 18th centuries.</p><p>The articles of the 1790 diet provided that it meet at least every three years, but since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy summoned it, the promise was often broken. It was reconstituted in 1867 after the Austro-Hungarian Compromise. The term Natio Hungarica, the Hungarian nation, designated the political elite who took part in the diet, consisting of the nobility, the Catholic clergy and a few enfranchised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>, regardless of language or ethnicity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Under communist rule the Assembly was defined as the supreme body of state power and, under the Constitution of 1949, was the sole branch of government on paper. In practice it ratified decisions already taken by the Hungarian Socialist Workers' Party and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> and sat only twice a year. Between sessions the Presidential Council exercised most of its powers and could issue edicts in place of laws.</p><p>The liberal democratic character of parliament was restored with the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-curtain">Iron Curtain</a> and the end of communist rule in 1989. The present body is unicameral, with 199 members elected to four-year terms, and it has met in the Hungarian Parliament Building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> since 1902. It keeps the old name Országgyűlés but is called the National Assembly to distance it from the royal diet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Assembly (Hungary)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
